<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pauline epistles</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 48–65 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Epistles of Paul, Letters of Paul</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pauline epistles are the thirteen books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle">Paul the Apostle</a>, among the earliest extant Christian documents. Most scholars believe Paul wrote seven of them: Galatians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, 1 and 2 Corinthians, Philemon, Philippians and 1 Thessalonians, with consensus dates from about 48 CE for Galatians. Scholars are about evenly divided on 2 Thessalonians, Colossians and Ephesians, and many regard 1 and 2 Timothy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-saint">Titus</a> as written in his name by others. The Epistle to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrews</a> was traditionally counted as Paul's, but few scholars now ascribe it to him. They are foundational texts for Christian theology and ethics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pauline-epistles.jpg" alt="Pauline epistles" width="330" height="483" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ann_Arbor,_University_of_Michigan_P.Mich.inv._6238_(Papyrus_46)_fol._158r_Eph_6,_20–Gal_1,_8.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In every one of these letters except Hebrews the writer claims to be Paul. Scholars who doubt the authorship of some rely mostly on internal evidence such as style, vocabulary and subject matter, which tends to be subjective, while defenders point to external evidence: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, early canonical lists and reception by ancient authors. Paul states in several letters that he used secretaries, sometimes named, and Candida Moss has argued that enslaved and formerly enslaved secretaries played a significant but overlooked part in producing early Christian texts.</p><p>The letters follow the conventional Greco-Roman structure with some modification: opening, thanksgiving or blessing, body and closing. David Trobisch finds it likely that Paul first collected his letters for publication himself, as other ancient letter writers did, and the collection circulated separately before it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The letters stand in a consistent order, apparently by descending length of the Greek text, with the three pastoral epistles addressed to individuals kept together at the end. Hebrews, which is anonymous, follows them in modern editions, a placement that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerome">Jerome</a>'s Vulgate popularised. Its place varies in older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">Papyrus</a> 46 puts it between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and 1 Corinthians, while others place it after 2 Thessalonians or after Philemon.</p><p>Paul's own writings are sometimes thought to point to letters that were not preserved: a first letter to Corinth, possibly referred to at 1 Corinthians 5:9, a severe letter written between 1 and 2 Corinthians, and a possible letter to the Laodiceans, mentioned at Colossians 4:16.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The gospels and Paul's letters were the core of what became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>. Other writings were later attributed to Paul and are now considered pseudepigraphic. The Third Epistle to the Corinthians was considered genuine for a time by the Syriac Orthodox Church and the Armenian Apostolic Church and is now dated to the second half of the 2nd century. The Muratorian fragment denounces an Epistle to the Alexandrians and a Marcionite Epistle to the Laodiceans as forgeries by Marcion of Sinope, and a supposed correspondence between Paul and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-the-younger">Seneca the Younger</a> is universally considered a forgery of the 4th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pauline epistles” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
